---
title: Mobility progression
---

A mobility progression algorithm decides your next targets for a **stretching /
mobility drill**, given its history — the stretching counterpart of a
[progression algorithm](/docs/plugins/progression). The user picks one in
**Settings → Mobility progression**; the app calls it whenever a Mobility block
needs a suggestion, and persists the returned `nextState` per drill after a
session.

The key difference from strength progression: a drill is measured by **hold
time** or by **reps** (± load), and unilateral drills progress **each side
independently** — so sets and history carry an optional `side`.

## The contract

```ts
export const pluginBundle = {
  formatVersion: 1,
  pluginId: "com.you.my-mobility",
  family: "mobility-progression",
  entryExport: "algorithm",
} as const;

export const algorithm = {
  id: "com.you.my-mobility",
  name: "My Mobility Scheme",
  description: "Add 5s to a hold, or a rep, each session you meet the target.",
  defaultState: {},

  suggest(input) {
    const isHold = input.drill.metric === "hold";
    const sides = input.drill.perSide ? ["left", "right"] : [null];
    const last = input.history[0];

    const sets = [];
    for (const side of sides) {
      const sideSets = (last?.sets ?? []).filter((s) => (side ? s.side === side : !s.side));
      const best = Math.max(0, ...sideSets.map((s) => (isHold ? s.durationSec : s.reps) ?? 0));
      const next = isHold ? (best || 15) + 5 : (best || 5) + 1;
      for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        sets.push(side
          ? (isHold ? { side, durationSec: next } : { side, reps: next })
          : (isHold ? { durationSec: next } : { reps: next }));
      }
    }
    return { sets, nextState: {}, label: `Try ${isHold ? "+5s" : "+1 rep"}` };
  },
};
```

## Input — `MobilityProgressionInput`

See `@logit/core/domain/mobilityProgression`.

| Field | Notes |
|---|---|
| `drill` | `{ name, id?, metric: "hold" \| "reps", perSide, area? }` |
| `history` | `MobilityHistoryEntry[]`, **most recent first**. Each entry's `sets` carry `side?`, `durationSec?`, `reps?`, `loadKg?`, `targetSec?`, `depth?` (1–5), `completed?`. |
| `state` | Your opaque value — whatever you returned as `nextState` last time. Starts as `defaultState`. |
| `userPreferences` | Values the user set, if you declare a `preferencesSchema`. |
| `plannedTargets` | `{ sets?, durationSec?, reps?, loadKg? }` from a template, if any. |
| `now` | Epoch ms, passed in so the algorithm stays pure. |

## Output — `MobilityProgressionOutput`

```ts
type MobilityProgressionOutput = {
  sets: { side?: "left" | "right"; durationSec?: number; reps?: number; loadKg?: number; note?: string }[];
  nextState: unknown;   // persisted by the app, handed back next call
  label?: string;       // shown in the block header, e.g. "Last: 45s L · 40s R → try 50s"
  notes?: string;
};
```

Prescribe one entry per set (per side, for unilateral drills). The app prefills
each logged set's `targetSec` from your `durationSec` so the hold timer has a
goal.

## Preferences, build & test

Identical to [progression algorithms](/docs/plugins/progression): export a
`preferencesSchema`, bundle with esbuild, test `suggest` as a pure function, then
[publish](/docs/plugins/publishing). Sample:
`static/sample-plugins/mobility-plus`.
